{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DESIGN ADJUDICATION (independent verification session) — NOTHING CHANGED: no AC ticked, no description edit, no --force, ticket left in_progress. This comment is the only write I made. I was dispatched to close this ticket, verified the collision flagged in the comment above independently, and stopped.\n\nWHAT I RE-VERIFIED FIRST-HAND (measurement, not inference)\n\n1. `compaction_child_repo_root` exists NOWHERE on origin/main @ 25ea12c200ae: `git grep -c compaction_child_repo_root origin/main -- .` exits 1 (no match). The current AC#1/AC#4 therefore describe a symbol that is not in the tree. Confirmed: ticking them would be false. I did not.\n\n2. `_proc.detached_child_cwd` MEASURED, not assumed. Built the real topology — a main checkout with a durable `.tickets-tracker`, an ephemeral `git worktree` whose `.tickets-tracker` is a SYMLINK into it — and called the real helper: it anchors at the durable main checkout, escaping the ephemeral worktree. Source agrees (`_proc.py:141`, `os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(tracker))`, with realpath load-bearing per bugs 3198/93a9).\n\n3. The merged fix's FALLBACK arm, pinned AT THE SEAM. With `REBAR_ROOT` unset, that real symlink store, and the cwd set to the real `detached_child_cwd` anchor, `run_sweep(symlink_tracker)` hands `compact_all_cli` the durable main checkout — not the ephemeral worktree, not `dirname(symlink)`. Mutation-checked: `repo_root=None` and `repo_root=os.path.dirname(tracker)` both FAIL it. Worth noting because the in-repo AC#4 test (`::test_run_sweep_resolves_the_code_root_without_rebar_root`) is end-to-end only and SURVIVES the `repo_root=None` mutant — only `::test_run_sweep_hands_the_resolved_code_root_to_the_sweep` kills it, and that test runs the REBAR_ROOT arm. So the fallback arm's seam pin is real behaviour but is not yet asserted in the repo suite.\n\n4. Full mutant matrix against merged `c68cc1de3185` (each applied then reverted; tree verified clean after):\n   - `dirname(tracker)` (re-seed the defect): 3 of 4 run_sweep tests FAIL. Killed.\n   - `None`: only the seam test fails. Killed by the seam test alone.\n   - `dirname(os.path.realpath(tracker))`: 3 of 4 FAIL. Killed. — see point 6, this one matters.\n   Suite green as merged: 51 passed across the three touched modules; `scripts/check_repo_root_from_tracker.py` exits 0; the guard self-test is non-vacuous (re-adding a `# repo-root-ok:` marker to compact_trigger.py makes it FAIL).\n\nTHE DESIGN QUESTION, ON THE MERITS\n\nThe critique of the merged design is legitimate and I confirm its factual basis: `repo_root_or_none()`'s non-`REBAR_ROOT` arm reads git-toplevel-of-CWD, so local correctness depends on `detached_child_cwd` continuing to anchor the child. And `run_sweep` does have a SYNCHRONOUS caller — `maybe_compact` calls `run_sweep(tracker)` inline when `compact.trigger == \"always\"` (tests/CI) — where no anchoring applies and the cwd is simply the caller's. That is a real non-local coupling and it is uneven.\n\nBut two verified facts decide it against the proposed replacement:\n\n5. THE REPLACEMENT'S FALLBACK ARM REGRESSES A CASE THE MERGED CODE GETS RIGHT. `dirname(canonical tracker)` is operationally mutant 3 above, and mutant 3 FAILS `::test_run_sweep_resolves_the_code_root_without_rebar_root` — a relocated store with `REBAR_ROOT` UNSET and the cwd inside the checkout. There, `repo_root_or_none()` resolves the checkout correctly while `dirname(canonical tracker)` yields the external store's parent: the ORIGINAL BUG, reproduced. The proposed design is correct only while `REBAR_ROOT` is exported for every relocated store; the merged one is correct whenever EITHER `REBAR_ROOT` is set OR the cwd is in the checkout — a strictly wider envelope. Note this also answers the synchronous-caller objection in the merged design's favour: in the inline `trigger == \"always\"` path the caller's cwd is inside the code checkout, which is exactly where git-toplevel is RIGHT and `dirname(canonical tracker)` is WRONG.\n\n6. THE REPLACEMENT WOULD BE INVISIBLE TO THE GATE, NOT MERELY SANCTIONED. I probed `scripts/check_repo_root_from_tracker.py` with both forms in one file under `src/`. It flags `os.path.dirname(tracker)` and does NOT flag `os.path.dirname(StorePaths(tracker).canonical)` — the detector matches the bare name `tracker`, a `*.tracker_dir()` call, or those wrapped in `str()`, and deliberately does not unwrap the realpath/abspath family (its own docstring says so; that family is split to flowered-basaltic-beagle). So the proposed fallback would need no `# repo-root-ok:` sanction at all. That is worse than re-populating the allowlist: a composing site of exactly the class this gate exists to catch would sit in the tree unflagged and unsanctioned, while AC#3 truthfully reports \"zero deferred config-root sites\". The guard's own remedy text names `config.repo_root_or_none()` as a sanctioned fix — the merged code follows the gate's stated guidance.\n\nRECOMMENDATION — disposition (a): ACCEPT WHAT LANDED.\n\n`c68cc1de3185` carries LLM-Review+1 and Verified+1, is merged, drains the last sanctioned site, and has the wider correctness envelope. The stale artefact is the AC text, not the code. Reconcile AC#1 and AC#4 to the merged design (keeping the superseded text visible as a note, so the pivot stays auditable), then tick and close. AC#2a/#2b/#3/#5/#6 already match the tree exactly and are evidenced above; AC#6's culprit is confirmed — `d2dea317ec94` carries `rebar-ticket: 1fde-becd-942a-4351` and introduced the `repo_root = os.path.dirname(tracker)` line, and the `caused_by` link to 1fde-becd-942a-4351 is present with provenance=explicit.\n\nAgainst disposition (b): landing the resolver as a refactor on top would narrow the correctness envelope (point 5) and reintroduce an undetectable composing construct (point 6). If the cwd coupling is judged unacceptable on its own terms, the right fix is not `dirname(canonical tracker)` but making the child's root explicit without composing it from the store — a separate design ticket, not a change to this one's ACs.\n\nTWO FOLLOW-UPS WORTH FILING (not filed by me; I am not the ticket's owner this session):\n  - Seam-pin the fallback arm in the repo suite: `::test_run_sweep_resolves_the_code_root_without_rebar_root` is end-to-end only and survives `repo_root=None`. My verification script above is the shape it should take.\n  - Decide whether the guard should unwrap `StorePaths(...).canonical` as a store shape, so a future `dirname(canonical tracker)` cannot enter the tree unflagged (point 6). Possibly belongs to flowered-basaltic-beagle (088b-e4c1-050c-4516).\n"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787958829677167001,"uuid":"f1dd605e-cbd9-4c1b-8f89-14a76e4936a8"}